        email:   notmain@email.com
        -->

        ...

        ### Section __Title__

        ...

      It is possible to add styling elements to the titles, but this should be used only in some rare cases.
    """
    'descriptionMoreURL': 'https://github.com/adam-p/markdown-here/wiki/Markdown-Cheatsheet'
    'leftLabelHTML': '<span style="color:#ff0">🗂</span>'


  '#### SubSection Title':
    'prefix': 'lia-title-4'
    'body': '#### ${1:SubSection Title}\n'
    'description': """
      As in common Markdown, a document is segregated into different parts by its titles. A title starts with hash-tag (#) that is followed by an head-line. The number of subsequent hash-tags defines the order. It is furthermore possible to overwrite some of the definitions from the main header, see more information on this by typing "liaheadersection" ...

      Example:
        # Main Title

        ...

        ## Chapter Title
        <!--
        comment: This comment is only for demonstrating purposes
                 and will be overwritten within the next slide...

        author:  New Author
        email:   notmain@email.com
        -->

        ...

        ### Section __Title__

        ...

      It is possible to add styling elements to the titles, but this should be used only in some rare cases.
    """
    'descriptionMoreURL': 'https://github.com/adam-p/markdown-here/wiki/Markdown-Cheatsheet'
    'leftLabelHTML': '<span style="color:#ff0">🗂</span>'


  '##### Paragraph Title':
    'prefix': 'lia-title-5'
    'body': '##### ${1:Paragraph Title}\n'
    'description': """
      As in common Markdown, a document is segregated into different parts by its titles. A title starts with hash-tag (#) that is followed by an head-line. The number of subsequent hash-tags defines the order. It is furthermore possible to overwrite some of the definitions from the main header, see more information on this by typing "liaheadersection" ...

      Example:
        # Main Title

        ...

        ## Chapter Title
        <!--
        comment: This comment is only for demonstrating purposes
                 and will be overwritten within the next slide...

        author:  New Author
        email:   notmain@email.com
        -->

        ...

        ### Section __Title__

        ...

      It is possible to add styling elements to the titles, but this should be used only in some rare cases.
    """
    'descriptionMoreURL': 'https://github.com/adam-p/markdown-here/wiki/Markdown-Cheatsheet'
    'leftLabelHTML': '<span style="color:#ff0">🗂</span>'


  '###### SubParagraph Title':
    'prefix': 'lia-title-6'
    'body': '###### ${1:SubParagraph Title}\n'
    'description': """
      As in common Markdown, a document is segregated into different parts by its titles. A title starts with hash-tag (#) that is followed by an head-line. The number of subsequent hash-tags defines the order. It is furthermore possible to overwrite some of the definitions from the main header, see more information on this by typing "liaheadersection" ...

      Example:
        # Main Title

        ...

        ## Chapter Title
        <!--
        comment: This comment is only for demonstrating purposes
                 and will be overwritten within the next slide...

        author:  New Author
        email:   notmain@email.com
        -->

        ...

        ### Section __Title__

        ...

      It is possible to add styling elements to the titles, but this should be used only in some rare cases.
    """
    'descriptionMoreURL': 'https://github.com/adam-p/markdown-here/wiki/Markdown-Cheatsheet'
    'leftLabelHTML': '<span style="color:#ff0">🗂</span>'

  '<details><summary>...':
    'prefix': 'lia-details'
    'body': """
      <details>

      <summary>${1:title}</summary>

      ${2:body}

      </details>
    """
    'description': """
      The details and summary tags are standard HTML tags and GitHub also supports their usage with Markdown. These tags offer a neat way to define something what is nowadays called accordion. Thus, your user can click on the summary text to make the body of the details-tag appear.

      Example:
        <details>

        <summary>**Honest Textbook ads (click to enlarge)**</summary>

        !?[If High School and College Textbooks Were Honest - Honest Ads](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lhSjYT7pWkw)

        </details>
    """
    'descriptionMoreURL': 'https://github.com/adam-p/markdown-here/wiki/Markdown-Cheatsheet'
    'leftLabelHTML': '<span style="color:#ff0">🗂</span>'

  '> [!NOTE]':
    'prefix': 'lia-alert-note'
    'body': """
      > [!NOTE]${1}
      > ${2:Useful information that users should know, even when skimming the content.}
    """
    'description': """
      GitHub-style alert box: a blockquote that starts with [!NOTE]. Useful information that users should know, even when skimming the content. An optional title or emoji can follow the keyword on the same line (GitLab style). The alert renders as a normal quote in other Markdown viewers.

      Available types: NOTE, TIP, IMPORTANT, WARNING, CAUTION

      Example:
        > [!NOTE]
        > Useful information that users should know, even when skimming the content.

        > [!NOTE] ℹ️ Custom title
        > The title replaces the default one.
    """
    'descriptionMoreURL': 'https://liascript.github.io/course/?https://raw.githubusercontent.com/liaScript/docs/master/README.md'
    'leftLabelHTML': '<span style="color:#ff0">ℹ️</span>'


  '> [!TIP]':
    'prefix': 'lia-alert-tip'
    'body': """
      > [!TIP]${1}
      > ${2:Helpful advice for doing things better or more easily.}
    """
    'description': """
      GitHub-style alert box: a blockquote that starts with [!TIP]. Helpful advice for doing things better or more easily. An optional title or emoji can follow the keyword on the same line (GitLab style). The alert renders as a normal quote in other Markdown viewers.

      Available types: NOTE, TIP, IMPORTANT, WARNING, CAUTION

      Example:
        > [!TIP]
        > Helpful advice for doing things better or more easily.

        > [!TIP] 💡 Custom title
        > The title replaces the default one.
    """
    'descriptionMoreURL': 'https://liascript.github.io/course/?https://raw.githubusercontent.com/liaScript/docs/master/README.md'
    'leftLabelHTML': '<span style="color:#ff0">💡</span>'


  '> [!IMPORTANT]':
    'prefix': 'lia-alert-important'
    'body': """
      > [!IMPORTANT]${1}
      > ${2:Key information users need to know to achieve their goal.}
    """
    'description': """
      GitHub-style alert box: a blockquote that starts with [!IMPORTANT]. Key information users need to know to achieve their goal. An optional title or emoji can follow the keyword on the same line (GitLab style). The alert renders as a normal quote in other Markdown viewers.

      Available types: NOTE, TIP, IMPORTANT, WARNING, CAUTION

      Example:
        > [!IMPORTANT]
        > Key information users need to know to achieve their goal.

        > [!IMPORTANT] ❗ Custom title
        > The title replaces the default one.
    """
    'descriptionMoreURL': 'https://liascript.github.io/course/?https://raw.githubusercontent.com/liaScript/docs/master/README.md'
    'leftLabelHTML': '<span style="color:#ff0">❗</span>'


  '> [!WARNING]':
    'prefix': 'lia-alert-warning'
    'body': """
      > [!WARNING]${1}
      > ${2:Urgent information that needs immediate attention to avoid problems.}
    """
    'description': """
      GitHub-style alert box: a blockquote that starts with [!WARNING]. Urgent information that needs immediate attention to avoid problems. An optional title or emoji can follow the keyword on the same line (GitLab style). The alert renders as a normal quote in other Markdown viewers.

      Available types: NOTE, TIP, IMPORTANT, WARNING, CAUTION

      Example:
        > [!WARNING]
        > Urgent information that needs immediate attention to avoid problems.

        > [!WARNING] ⚠️ Custom title
        > The title replaces the default one.
    """
    'descriptionMoreURL': 'https://liascript.github.io/course/?https://raw.githubusercontent.com/liaScript/docs/master/README.md'
    'leftLabelHTML': '<span style="color:#ff0">⚠️</span>'


  '> [!CAUTION]':
    'prefix': 'lia-alert-caution'
    'body': """
      > [!CAUTION]${1}
      > ${2:Advice about risks or negative outcomes of certain actions.}
    """
    'description': """
      GitHub-style alert box: a blockquote that starts with [!CAUTION]. Advice about risks or negative outcomes of certain actions. An optional title or emoji can follow the keyword on the same line (GitLab style). The alert renders as a normal quote in other Markdown viewers.

      Available types: NOTE, TIP, IMPORTANT, WARNING, CAUTION

      Example:
        > [!CAUTION]
        > Advice about risks or negative outcomes of certain actions.

        > [!CAUTION] 🛑 Custom title
        > The title replaces the default one.
    """
    'descriptionMoreURL': 'https://liascript.github.io/course/?https://raw.githubusercontent.com/liaScript/docs/master/README.md'
    'leftLabelHTML': '<span style="color:#ff0">🛑</span>'


  '<section> ... </section>':
    'prefix': 'lia-section'
    'body': """
      <section>

      ${1:## Grouped Sub-Heading}

      ${2:Content that stays on this slide.}

      </section>
    """
    'description': """
      Every heading (#, ##, ###, ...) starts a new slide. To use headings inside a slide without creating new slides, wrap them in a <section> or <article> tag. Leave blank lines around the content, so that it is parsed as Markdown.

      Example:
        # Slide Title

        <section>

        ## Part A

        Some text.

        </section>
    """
    'descriptionMoreURL': 'https://liascript.github.io/course/?https://raw.githubusercontent.com/liaScript/docs/master/README.md'
    'leftLabelHTML': '<span style="color:#ff0">#</span>'


  'Title ===== (setext)':
    'prefix': 'lia-title-setext'
    'body': """
      ${1:Local Subsection}
      ${2:====================}
    """
    'description': """
      Setext-style headings (text underlined with === or ---) do not start a new slide and do not appear in the table of contents. Use them for local structure within a slide.

        ===   -> large local heading
        ---   -> smaller local heading

      Example:
        ## Section Title

        Local Subsection
        ================

        Local Sub-Subsection
        --------------------
    """
    'descriptionMoreURL': 'https://liascript.github.io/course/?https://raw.githubusercontent.com/liaScript/docs/master/README.md'
    'leftLabelHTML': '<span style="color:#ff0">#</span>'


  '<lia-keep> ... </lia-keep>':
    'prefix': 'lia-keep'
    'body': """
      <lia-keep>
      ${1:<table>
        <tr><td rowspan="2">raw</td><td>HTML</td></tr>
        <tr><td>without Markdown parsing</td></tr>
      </table>}
      </lia-keep>
    """
    'description': """
      Everything inside <lia-keep> is passed through as raw HTML: no Markdown parsing and no indentation checks. Useful for complex hand-written HTML, such as tables with rowspan or colspan.

      Example:
        <lia-keep>
        <table>
          <tr><td rowspan="2">A</td><td>B</td></tr>
          <tr><td>C</td></tr>
        </table>
        </lia-keep>
    """
    'descriptionMoreURL': 'https://liascript.github.io/course/?https://raw.githubusercontent.com/liaScript/docs/master/README.md'
    'leftLabelHTML': '<span style="color:#ff0">&lt;/&gt;</span>'


  '<!--- ignored comment --->':
    'prefix': 'lia-comment-ignore'
    'body': """
      <!---
      ${1:This note is ignored by LiaScript.}
      --->
    """
    'description': """
      A comment that starts with three or more dashes (<!--- ... --->) is dropped completely by LiaScript. A normal HTML comment (<!-- ... -->) is read as attributes for the next element. Use it for notes to yourself or to comment out content.

      Important: always leave a blank line between this comment and any other HTML comment (e.g. a styling or chart comment). Two comments without a blank line are merged by the Markdown parser and the following element is shown as plain text.

      Example:
        <!---
        TODO: add a better picture here
        --->

        <!-- data-type="LinePlot" -->
        | x | y |
        |---|---|
        | 1 | 2 |
    """
    'descriptionMoreURL': 'https://liascript.github.io/course/?https://raw.githubusercontent.com/liaScript/docs/master/README.md'
    'leftLabelHTML': '<span style="color:#ff0">🙈</span>'


  '<!-- style="..." --> block':
    'prefix': 'lia-style-block'
    'body': """
      <!-- style="${1:color: purple; font-size: 1.2em;}" ${2:class="${3:animated rollIn}"} -->
      ${4:This whole paragraph is styled.}
    """
    'description': """
      Style any block (paragraph, list, table, quote, code, ...) by placing an HTML comment with attributes directly above it. Any HTML attribute works, e.g. style, class or id. The comment is invisible in other Markdown viewers.

      Example:
        <!-- style="color: purple; font-size: 1.2em" class="animated rollIn" -->
        This whole paragraph is styled.

        <!-- style="width: 50%" -->
        | <!-- style="background: azure" --> Header 1 | Header 2 |
        |:--------------------------------------------|:---------|
        | <!-- style="background: coral" --> Item 1   | Item 2   |
    """
    'descriptionMoreURL': 'https://liascript.github.io/course/?https://raw.githubusercontent.com/liaScript/docs/master/README.md'
    'leftLabelHTML': '<span style="color:#ff0">🎨</span>'


  'inline<!-- style="..." -->':
    'prefix': 'lia-style-inline'
    'body': '<!-- style="${1:color: red;}" -->'
    'description': """
      Style an inline element (bold text, a link, an image, a formula, ...) by placing an HTML comment with attributes directly after it, without a space.

      Example:
        This **is important**<!-- style="color: red" --> text.

        ![a lion](lion.svg)<!-- style="width: 300px;" class="animated bounce" -->
    """
    'descriptionMoreURL': 'https://liascript.github.io/course/?https://raw.githubusercontent.com/liaScript/docs/master/README.md'
    'leftLabelHTML': '<span style="color:#ff0">🎨</span>'
